---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is the random student picker really random?', a: 'Yes. It uses your browser\'s built-in secure random number generator, and every name that is still in the pool has the same chance of being chosen.' },
  { q: 'Can the same student be picked twice?', a: 'Not while "Do not repeat until everyone is picked" is ticked. Once every name has been picked, press "Start over" to reset. Untick the box if repeats are fine.' },
  { q: 'Is the class list saved or uploaded?', a: 'No. Names stay in your browser tab and are never sent anywhere. If you refresh the page they disappear, so keep your list in a document.' },
  { q: 'How do I make random groups?', a: 'Use the group maker below the picker. Choose a number of groups or a group size, press Make groups, and the class is shuffled and divided evenly.' },
  { q: 'Can I pick more than one student at once?', a: 'Yes. Set "How many to pick" to any number up to 50 and press the button to get several names in one go.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout slug="random-student-picker" faqs={faqs}>
  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: lead paragraph, drafted by AI. */}
  <p slot="lead">
    This random student picker chooses names fairly from your class list, never repeating a student until everyone
    has had a turn. It can also split the class into random groups. The list stays in your browser.
  </p>

  <div slot="tool">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="rp-names">Class list (one name per line)</label>
      <textarea id="rp-names" placeholder="Asha Verma&#10;Rahul Singh&#10;Meena Iyer&#10;Kabir Khan"></textarea>
      <p id="rp-remain" class="hint">0 students in the list</p>
    </div>
    <div class="row two">
      <div class="field"><label for="rp-count">How many to pick</label><input id="rp-count" type="number" min="1" max="50" value="1" inputmode="numeric" /></div>
      <div class="field" style="align-self:end"><label><input id="rp-norepeat" type="checkbox" checked /> Do not repeat until everyone is picked</label></div>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <button type="button" id="rp-pick" class="btn">Pick a student</button>
      <button type="button" id="rp-reset" class="btn secondary">Start over</button>
    </div>
    <div class="result" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="picked" id="rp-shown">–</p>
      <p id="rp-status" class="status" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div hidden><h3>Picked so far</h3><ol id="rp-history"></ol></div>

    <h3>Make random groups</h3>
    <div class="row three">
      <div class="field"><label for="rp-gmode">Divide by</label><select id="rp-gmode"><option value="count">Number of groups</option><option value="size">Students per group</option></select></div>
      <div class="field"><label for="rp-gsize" id="rp-glabel">Number of groups</label><input id="rp-gsize" type="number" min="1" max="100" value="4" inputmode="numeric" /></div>
      <div class="field" style="align-self:end"><button type="button" id="rp-gmake" class="btn">Make groups</button></div>
    </div>
    <div id="rp-groups" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: how-to and worked example drafted by AI. Add how you use random picking in your own class. */}
  <h2>How to use the random student picker</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste or type your class list, one name on each line.</li>
    <li>Choose how many students to pick. Leave the repeat box ticked so everyone gets a turn.</li>
    <li>Press Pick a student. The name appears after a short shuffle. Picked names are listed below.</li>
    <li>When all names are used, press Start over. To make teams, use the group maker instead.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p class="worked">
    Your class has 30 students and you want to ask five of them to read aloud. Paste the list, set "How many to pick" to
    5 and press the button. Five different names appear. On the next day press it again: those five will not be chosen
    until the other 25 have had a turn. For a lab, set the group maker to 6 groups and you get six teams of five.
  </p>
  <h2>Why random picking helps</h2>
  <p>
    Teachers tend to call on the same hands again and again without noticing. A random picker removes that habit and
    shows students that everyone may be asked. Nothing here is stored, so remove any names you prefer not to type.
  </p>
  <script>import '../../scripts/picker.js';</script>
</ToolLayout>
